<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hecatompylos</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Shahr-e Qumis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hecatompylos, Greek for 'hundred gates', was an ancient city in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, identified with the ruins of Shahr-e Qumis near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damghan">Damghan</a>. It lay on the main road from Media to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, was taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> in 330 BCE, and served as an early capital and royal residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a>.</p></div>
